# XSH getenv(), secure_getenv()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The tests use names beginning with PCX_, which no utility or
# implementation uses (lowercase names are reserved for applications by
# XBD 8.1, but the case test needs an uppercase one).

assertion  XSH.getenv.001
anchor     functions/getenv.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getenv.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.1
class      required
since      1988
says       In a process started by execve() with PCX_A=one, PCX_AB=two and
           PCX_E= (empty value) in its environment, getenv("PCX_A")
           returns "one", getenv("PCX_AB") returns "two", getenv("PCX_E")
           returns an empty string (not a null pointer), and
           getenv("PCX_Z"), getenv("PCX_") and getenv("pcx_a") return
           null pointers (a name is matched whole, and upper and lower
           case are not folded together).
note       The test runs itself again through execve() with its own path
           (argv[0]) and an environment of its own plus the harness's
           PC_* and LD_PRELOAD entries.
test       t/001-initial-environment.c

assertion  XSH.getenv.002
anchor     functions/getenv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       After setenv("PCX_A", "one", 1), the pointer getenv("PCX_A")
           returns lies within one of the strings of the array environ
           points to, and the string there is "one" (not a copy in a
           buffer of the implementation's own).
test       t/002-points-into-environ.c

assertion  XSH.getenv.003
anchor     functions/getenv.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.1
class      required
says       After getenv() has been used, assigning environ a new array
           (the old entries without PCX_A, plus "PCX_N=new") changes what
           getenv() finds: getenv("PCX_N") returns "new" and
           getenv("PCX_A") a null pointer; assigning environ back to an
           array that holds "PCX_A=again" makes getenv("PCX_A") return
           "again".
note       An implementation may copy the new array and its strings when
           it notices the assignment (getenv() RATIONALE), so the test
           does not look at where the result points.
test       t/003-environ-assigned.c

assertion  XSH.getenv.004
anchor     functions/getenv.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getenv.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       secure_getenv() of a name not in the environment returns a
           null pointer; for PCX_A=one it returns either a null pointer
           (only an implementation-defined criterion allows this when the
           real and effective IDs were equal at startup: the result is
           then UNRESOLVED) or, like getenv(), a pointer to "one" within
           the environment data.
test       t/004-secure-getenv.c

assertion  XSH.getenv.005
anchor     functions/getenv.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       In a process whose effective user ID differed from its real
           user ID at startup (started by execve() after seteuid(65534)
           by root), secure_getenv("PCX_S") returns a null pointer
           although getenv("PCX_S") finds the variable; likewise when the
           effective group ID differed from the real group ID (after
           setegid(65534)).
note       With effective user ID 65534 the exec needs search permission
           on the path to the test and execute permission on it for that
           ID; the test is UNRESOLVED when the exec fails.
needs      root
nomutant   a process whose IDs differ at startup is in secure-execution mode, and the preload libraries that plant faults are not loaded into it; reviewed by hand
test       t/005-secure-getenv-ids.c
